Icepi Zero ADC/DAC tutorials: licences
=======================================

Copyright (c) 2026 Jason Gallicchio

This repository holds text, code, a circuit board and prebuilt binaries, and
each kind is under the licence that suits it. The full texts are in LICENSES/.

  What                                      Where                              Licence
  ----------------------------------------  ---------------------------------  ----------------
  The tutorial: text, figures, animations,  README.md, tutorial/ (pages and    CC BY 4.0
  photos and measured data                  img/), dev/data/, dev/README.md,
                                            dev/CLAUDE_CODE_CHAT.md, the
                                            other README.md files (adapter_board/
                                            README.md too; only its design files
                                            are CERN-OHL-P)

  The code: SystemVerilog, C, Python,       src/ (except the two lines below), MIT
  shell scripts, Makefiles, Buildroot and   dev/tools/
  kernel configuration

  The Linux driver                          src/linux/driver/adda.c            GPL-2.0-only

  The adapter board: KiCad design,          adapter_board/                     CERN-OHL-P-2.0
  gerbers, schematic, pin map, render

  Prebuilt bitstream and Linux images       src/linux/prebuilt/                each part's own:
                                                                               see below

The code printed on the tutorial's pages is copied from src/ by
dev/tools/sync_md.py, so it is the same MIT-licensed code: you may use it under
MIT, even though the page around it is CC BY.


CC BY 4.0: the tutorial (LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt)
------------------------------------------------
You may copy, adapt and share the tutorial, for any purpose, including in your
own course materials, as long as you give credit, link to the licence, and say
if you changed it. A suitable credit:

  "Icepi Zero ADC/DAC tutorials" by Jason Gallicchio,
  https://github.com/gallicchio/icepi-zero-dac-adc, CC BY 4.0

The material by others listed under "Third-party material" below keeps its own
terms.


MIT: the code (LICENSES/MIT.txt)
--------------------------------
Use it, change it and ship it, in anything, as long as the copyright notice
and the licence text go with substantial copies.


GPL-2.0-only: the Linux driver (LICENSES/GPL-2.0-only.txt)
---------------------------------------------------------
src/linux/driver/adda.c is a Linux kernel module, so it is under the kernel's
own licence (its first line says so: SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0, and
MODULE_LICENSE("GPL")). The kernel only lets GPL-compatible modules use most
of its interfaces.


CERN-OHL-P-2.0: the adapter board (LICENSES/CERN-OHL-P-2.0.txt)
----------------------------------------------------------------
The CERN Open Hardware Licence, permissive variant: make, sell and modify the
board freely, keeping the notices. Nothing obliges you to share your changes.


Prebuilt binaries: src/linux/prebuilt/
--------------------------------------
These are built from open-source projects under their own licences, as made
by the steps in tutorial/3_03_building_linux.md:

  icepi_zero_adda.bit    The FPGA's bitstream: the VexRiscv CPU (MIT), LiteX's
                         cores and BIOS (BSD-2-Clause), picolibc in the BIOS
                         (BSD-3-Clause and others), and this repository's
                         peripherals (MIT).
  images/, install/,     Linux 6.12 (GPL-2.0), BusyBox 1.37.0 (GPL-2.0),
  sdcard.img.xz          musl 1.2.6 (MIT), MicroPython 1.22.2 (MIT and others),
                         OpenSBI 1.3.1 (BSD-2-Clause), GCC's runtime library
                         (GPL-3.0 with the GCC Runtime Library Exception), and
                         adda.ko (GPL-2.0, above).

src/linux/prebuilt/legal-info-manifest.csv is Buildroot's list of every target
package, its version, licence and source archive (from `make legal-info`).

The GPL requires that whoever passes on GPL binaries also makes their
complete corresponding source available. For these images, that is the source
that Buildroot's `make legal-info` collects (the Linux kernel and BusyBox
sources, with the patches applied to them, and the configuration), from:

  - Buildroot 2026.02.3, with src/linux/configs/icepi_adda_defconfig and the
    rest of src/linux/ as its external tree,
  - linux-on-litex-vexriscv 05fc5e4 (BSD-2-Clause), whose kernel
    configuration and patches it uses,
  - LiteX fad9b3759 (BSD-2-Clause), for the bitstream and the device tree.

src/linux/legal/collect_source.sh fetches all of it, laid out as Buildroot's
`make legal-info` lays it out, and src/linux/legal/README.md says what is in
it and where a complete archive is published.


Third-party material
--------------------
  - The 3D render of the Icepi Zero (dev/data/icepi_zero_render.png, and the
    LED animations tutorial/img/anim_counter.gif and anim_adc_leds.gif drawn
    on it) is rendered from cheyao's Icepi Zero KiCad design,
    https://github.com/cheyao/icepi-zero, under the Solderpad Hardware License
    v2.1.
  - Tux, in tutorial/img/linux_files.png: Larry Ewing (lewing@isc.tamu.edu)
    and The GIMP, who allow its use provided they are acknowledged; SVG by Simon Budig
    and Garrett LeSage, via Wikimedia Commons.
  - src/linux/configs/icepi_adda_defconfig begins with
    linux-on-litex-vexriscv's own defconfig (BSD-2-Clause, Copyright (c)
    2019-2024 Linux-on-LiteX-VexRiscv Developers), with this tutorial's
    additions marked.

The tools the tutorial installs (OSS CAD Suite, LiteX, Buildroot and the rest)
are downloaded by you, not included here, and come with their own licences.
